The failing test is `org.apache.commons.lang3.StringUtilsEqualsIndexOfTest.testContainsAnyStringWithSupplementaryChars()`. The test is checking the behavior of the `StringUtils.containsAny` method for supplementary characters.

The current bug report mentions that the `StringUtils.containsAny` methods incorrectly match Unicode 2.0+ supplementary characters. It provides an example where the method fails to handle supplementary characters correctly.

Looking at the implementation of the `StringUtils.containsAny` method, we have two versions:
1. `public static boolean containsAny(CharSequence cs, String searchChars)`:
```java
    public static boolean containsAny(CharSequence cs, String searchChars) {
        if (searchChars == null) {
            return false;
        }
        return containsAny(cs, searchChars.toCharArray());
    }
```

2. `public static boolean containsAny(CharSequence cs, char[] searchChars)`:
```java
    public static boolean containsAny(CharSequence cs, char[] searchChars) {
        if (isEmpty(cs) || ArrayUtils.isEmpty(searchChars)) {
            return false;
        }
        int csLength = cs.length();
        int searchLength = searchChars.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < csLength; i++) {
            char ch = cs.charAt(i);
            for (int j = 0; j < searchLength; j++) {
                if (searchChars[j] == ch) {
                    // ch is a supplementary character
                    // ch is in the Basic Multilingual Plane
                    return true;
                }
            }
        }
        return false;
    }
```

Based on the description in the bug report and the test, it seems that the issue lies in how supplementary characters are handled within the `StringUtils.containsAny` method. The current implementation does not correctly handle Unicode 2.0+ supplementary characters, leading to incorrect matching.

To fix this issue, the `StringUtils.containsAny` method needs to be updated to properly handle Unicode 2.0+ supplementary characters.

Next, I will suggest the methods that should be fixed to address this bug.
```java
org.apache.commons.lang3.StringUtils.containsAny(java.lang.CharSequence, java.lang.String)
org.apache.commons.lang3.StringUtils.containsAny(java.lang.CharSequence, char[])
```

